# NOTES
# 1. "CNPEC" (used as architect_engineer default for every CGN-owned plant in this batch: Taipingling, Taishan, Yangjiang, and incorrectly Tianwan 5-6) could not be confirmed as a real, distinct CGN entity in any source checked this session. CGN's actual engineering/design arm is commonly referred to in industry literature as "China Nuclear Power Design Company" (CNPDC), which was not independently re-confirmed this session either. Recommend a dedicated follow-up pass to pin down CGN's correct AE subsidiary name and re-run architect_engineer verification across the whole CGN fleet, not just this batch - "CNPEC" looks like it may be a fabricated blend of CNNC's real "CNPE" (China Nuclear Power Engineering Co.) with CGN's ownership tag.
# 2. Tianwan units 5 & 6 (ACPR-1000) are misattributed to CGN (constructor + architect_engineer) in the source data. Wikipedia's Tianwan article explicitly names "China Nuclear Power Engineering, Ltd. (CNPE)" as constructor for units 5-6, and the whole Tianwan site is a CNNC/Jiangsu Nuclear Power Corp (JNPC)/Rosatom joint venture, not a CGN site - even though ACPR-1000 is a CGN co-developed design lineage. This is a real instance of the CNNC/CGN confusion flagged in the task brief, but running in the opposite direction from the described heuristic (a CNNC site defaulted to CGN, apparently because of the ACPR-1000 model name association with CGN's design family).
# 3. RPV manufacturer for the newer VVER-1200 units (Tianwan 7-8, Xudabao 3-4, both under 2018-era Rosatom export contracts) should likely be AEM-Technologies/Atommash (Volgodonsk), not Izhora Plants - Russian VVER RPV production shifted to Atommash after its 2015 restart. Izhora Plants remains a plausible/CONFIRMED default for the older Tianwan 1-4 VVER-1000 units (built/commissioned 1999-2018, pre-dating the shift).
# 4. Turbine supplier for the same newer VVER-1200 cooperation units (Tianwan 7-8, Xudabao 3-4) is Chinese-supplied, not Power Machines (LMZ) - Rosatom's contracts for these units cover the nuclear island only. Confirmed specifically as Shanghai Electric for Tianwan 7-8; the Xudabao 3-4 Chinese supplier could not be confirmed by name this session (marked WRONG with an unconfirmed corrected_value - re-verify before use).
# 5. Shidaowan Guohe One (CAP1400) ownership/development is SPIC, not CNNC as defaulted - this confirms the special case flagged in the task brief. Its turbine_supplier default (Dongfang Electric) is nonetheless CONFIRMED correct by coincidence/genuine sourcing (Dongfang built the CAP1400 LP turbine module regardless of SPIC vs CNNC ownership).
# 6. Taishan EPR: turbine_supplier=Alstom is CONFIRMED correct as-is (WNA explicitly splits turbine=Alstom vs generator=Dongfang Electric - do not "fix" this to Dongfang). rpv_manufacturer=Mitsubishi Heavy Industries is WRONG; MHI has no documented role at Taishan, the nuclear island/RPV came from Areva/Framatome.
# 7. Large fraction of UNVERIFIABLE verdicts (67/128) reflects genuinely thin English-language sourcing for turbine_supplier/rpv_manufacturer/architect_engineer at CGN-owned sites (Taipingling, Yangjiang, Zhangzhou-adjacent CGN pattern) - Wikipedia and power-technology.com pages for these plants document ownership and construction timelines but not equipment supply chains. A follow-up pass using Chinese-language sources or CGN/CNNC annual reports would likely resolve many of these.
# 8. WebSearch tool exhausted its session quota (200/200) early in this batch; all research after that point relied on WebFetch against Wikipedia, world-nuclear.org, and DuckDuckGo HTML search (with mixed success - Bing returned unusable generic results, Ecosia 403'd, DuckDuckGo occasionally served a CAPTCHA page instead of results). This constrained the depth of verification, particularly for constructor/AE names at CGN sites and for Guohe One's architect_engineer, which is asserted from general industry knowledge rather than a source fetched this session.
